<h3>Mechanical Security Measures</h3>
<p>Once the vehicle is positioned, the security protocol begins with individual steel wheel-lock chocks. These heavy-duty chocks are placed at specific contact points on the tires to immobilize them completely.</p>
<p>Unlike standard wedges, steel chocks provide a rigid barrier that prevents any micro-movement, ensuring the vehicle remains static even when the carrier encounters sharp turns or sudden braking. This is the first layer of defense against the dynamic forces generated during highway transit.</p>
<p>The second layer of security is provided by high-tensile ratchet lashing systems. These straps are anchored to reinforced points on the carrier deck and tightly secured around the vehicle’s wheel hubs or chassis points. The ratchet mechanism allows for extreme tensioning, creating a uniform pull that eliminates slack.</p>
<p>This prevents the vehicle from shifting, rolling, or bouncing during high-speed travel. The combination of steel chocks and high-tensile lashing ensures that the vehicle remains in a fixed position, protecting the undercarriage and body panels from impact damage.</p>
